UNIT carried_shape_type
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@6199e2cc8b75 cli/sir/v05.mjs carriedShapeType with the type reader of cli/sir/canon.mjs (parseType) — reproduce the code exactly, accidents included
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG carried_shape_type(shape: text) -> json
ENVELOPE shape is well-formed text (no lone surrogate). Every text is in scope: a shape that the rules below do not turn
  into a type throws, and a throw is an observation (its message is not graded).
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION Read the leading type expression of a CARRIED SHAPE declaration and return it as a canon type tree. The shape
  text is cut at its first semicolon, the arrow spelling map K -> V is rewritten to map[K, V], the fixed-width integer
  names in type position are widened to the two canonical integer kinds, and the result is parsed by the SIR type grammar
  (TABLE TREE says the node shapes). The arrow and width rewrites are TEXTUAL: they happen before parsing, on the text,
  not on the tree. One whitespace set, WS (space, tab, CR, LF), serves the rewrites and the parse alike.
BEHAVIOR
R1 CUT: the segment is the text before the first semicolon of shape (all of shape when it has none); the rest is ignored.
R2 NORMALIZE(s): strip every leading and trailing WS character of s. If the stripped text begins with the three
  characters map, then one or more WS characters, and somewhere after that run the two characters -> occur at least
  one character past the end of the run, then K = the text from the end of the run up to the FIRST such ->, V = all text
  after that ->, and NORMALIZE(s) is the text map[ + NORMALIZE(K) + ", " + NORMALIZE(V) + ]. Otherwise NORMALIZE(s) is
  the stripped text unchanged. The rewrite recurses into K and V, so a value side map b -> c nests (map a -> map b -> c is
  map[a, map[b, c]]); a key side can only nest in the bracket spelling, since its first -> always ends K. map is lowercase
  and the run after it is WS only (a CR or LF there is a separator like a space): map[, mapX, map followed by U+00A0 or
  U+000B are not the arrow form. When ->
  directly follows the run, the split still happens (with an empty or arrow-led K) and the result always throws in R5.
  CITED FIDELITY package — lazy first-arrow split, v05.mjs:399
R3 The splice is textual: K and V are pasted into the bracket spelling unparsed, so a comma or bracket inside them
  changes the parse (map text, text -> u8 becomes map[text, text, u64] and throws; map text, text] | map[u8 -> text becomes
  map[text, text] | map[u64, text], a union of two maps). CITED FIDELITY package
R4 WIDEN: in the text NORMALIZE(segment), every TYPE-TOKEN equal to u8, u16 or u32 is replaced by u64, and every one equal
  to i8, i16 or i32 by i64 (lowercase only). A TYPE-TOKEN is an occurrence of one of those six spellings whose left side,
  after skipping WS, is the start of the text or one of the characters [ , | : and whose right side, after skipping WS,
  is one of ] , | or the end of the text. Everything else is untouched: a record field name (followed by :) keeps its
  spelling, so record[u8: u8] has a field named u8 of kind u64; u128, a_u8, u8x and U8 are not widened; a CR or LF in
  either gap is skipped like a space (list[u8] with an LF before its ] is list[u64]). CITED FIDELITY package — v05.mjs:400 token rule
R5 PARSE: the widened text must be exactly one TYPE of GRAMMAR (WS allowed before and after any token and at both
  ends; a word may not run into the next word). Anything else throws: an unknown word (including every name that R4 did
  not widen), a missing or extra bracket, comma or colon, an empty list of parts, trailing text, and any other
  whitespace character (U+00A0, U+000B, U+3000 …) anywhere.
R6 ARITY: list takes exactly one type, map exactly two, tuple one or more, record one or more fields; otherwise throw.
  Record field names within one record are distinct (exact text) or the parse throws.
R7 DEPTH: at most 16 composites (tuple, list, map, record) may be open at once; opening the 17th nested composite throws.
  Unions do not count. An arrow form counts as the map it becomes.
R8 UNREACHABLE ALTERNATIVE: for a union of alternatives A1 … An (in spelling order, n >= 2), the parse throws when some
  earlier Ai COVERS a later Aj. This check runs on every union, at any depth (a field type, a list item …). TABLE COVERS
  defines COVERS(a, b) (any row holding suffices) and TABLE JSONABLE defines JSONABLE(t). SAME(a, b) is structural
  equality in which a union's alternatives compare as a set (order ignored) and a record's fields compare as a set of
  (name, type) pairs (order ignored); tuple items keep order. Only earlier-covers-later is refused: text | json parses,
  json | text throws. CITED FIDELITY package — canon.mjs covers() / inJson()
R9 RESULT: the parsed type as the TREE node; a single alternative is its own node, two or more make a union node.
  Record fields and union alternatives keep spelling order.
TABLE COVERS COVERS(a, b) holds when any row holds
  | case | condition |
  | same | SAME(a, b) |
  | json | a is json and JSONABLE(b) |
  | list | a = list[x], b = list[y], COVERS(x, y) |
  | tuple | a and b tuples of equal length, COVERS(a item i, b item i) for every i |
  | map | a = map[k1, v1], b = map[k2, v2], COVERS(k1, k2) and COVERS(v1, v2) |
  | record | a and b records, and every field n: f of a has a field n: g in b (same name) with COVERS(f, g); b may have more fields |
  | a union | a is a union and some alternative of a COVERS b |
  | b union | b is a union and a COVERS every alternative of b |
TABLE JSONABLE JSONABLE(t) holds exactly when
  | t | condition |
  | none bool f64 text json | always |
  | list[x] | JSONABLE(x) |
  | tuple[x1, …] | every item JSONABLE |
  | map[k, x] | k is exactly text and JSONABLE(x) |
  | record[n1: x1, …] | every field type JSONABLE |
  | union | every alternative JSONABLE |
  | bytes i64 u64 | never |
TABLE TREE node shapes (a json map per node; key order immaterial)
  | type | node |
  | text bytes bool none i64 u64 f64 json | {"k": the word} |
  | list[T] | {"k": "list", "item": T} |
  | map[K, V] | {"k": "map", "key": K, "val": V} |
  | tuple[T1, …] | {"k": "tuple", "items": [T1, …]} |
  | record[n1: T1, …] | {"k": "record", "fields": [[n1, T1], …]} |
  | A1 pipe A2 pipe … | {"k": "union", "alts": [A1, A2, …]} |
PINNED grammar GRAMMAR = TYPE := ALT ( "|" ALT )* ; ALT := WORD-TYPE | "tuple" "[" TYPE ( "," TYPE )* "]" | "list" "[" TYPE "]" | "map" "[" TYPE "," TYPE "]" | "record" "[" IDENT ":" TYPE ( "," IDENT ":" TYPE )* "]" ; WORD-TYPE := "text" | "bytes" | "bool" | "none" | "i64" | "u64" | "f64" | "json" ; IDENT := [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]* CITED R5 R6 — canon.mjs reader; a type word is read as a whole IDENT first, then classified
PINNED charset WS = U+0020 U+0009 U+000D U+000A CITED R2 R4 R5 — the only whitespace of the trim, the arrow split, the width token rule and the reader
INVARIANT every non-throwing result is a TREE node whose every word is one of text bytes bool none i64 u64 f64 json; no
  u8, u16, u32, i8, i16 or i32 kind can appear (record field NAMES may still read u8 and the like).
ORACLE value -> oracles/carried_shape_type.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 abd1a223247808efe7fa77b63227576a39b7fc66438701edd566ee2ae73bacf4
SCOPE in-contract: R1–R9 on any well-formed text. Out of contract: the callers (rustCarriedTable's map check, the ts
  driver's own normalization), the error messages, and the non-text inputs the host would also refuse.
